 "standard_text": "Use function notation, evaluate functions for inputs in their domains, and interpret statements that use function notation in terms of a context.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra 1 function notation worksheets (F-IF.A.2): evaluate linear and quadratic functions, solve f(x) = k, use expressions such as f(a + 1) as inputs, combine function values and interpret f(x) in context. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "f(x) means the output of f when the input is x. Replace every x with the input.",
 "key_sub": "f(3) = 10 means input 3 gives output 10, so (3, 10) is on the graph.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) is read “<i>f</i> of <i>x</i>”. It means <b>the output of the function <i>f</i> when the input is <i>x</i></b>. It does not mean <i>f</i> times <i>x</i>.</p><p>To find <i>f</i>(3), replace <b>every</b> <i>x</i> with 3. Put negative inputs in parentheses: (−3)<sup>2</sup> = 9. The input can also be an expression: <i>f</i>(<i>a</i> + 1) means replace <i>x</i> with (<i>a</i> + 1).</p><p><i>f</i>(3) = 10 tells you the point (3, 10) is on the graph of <i>y</i> = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>).</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>If <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 3<i>x</i>, find <i>f</i>(−2).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2(−2)<sup>2</sup> − 3(−2) = 2(4) + 6.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 14</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>If <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 4<i>x</i> − 5, find <i>x</i> when <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 11.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4<i>x</i> − 5 = 11.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4<i>x</i> = 16.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 4</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>If <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 3<i>x</i> + 2, find <i>f</i>(<i>a</i> − 1).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3(<i>a</i> − 1) + 2 = 3<i>a</i> − 3 + 2.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3<i>a</i> − 1</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Reading <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) as “<i>f</i> times <i>x</i>”.</li><li>Squaring a negative input wrongly: for <i>x</i> = −3, <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> = 9, not −9.</li><li>Mixing up input and output: <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 3 asks for the input that gives 3, not for <i>f</i>(3).</li><li>Writing <i>f</i>(2<i>x</i>) as 2<i>f</i>(<i>x</i>), or <i>f</i>(<i>a</i> + 1) as <i>f</i>(<i>a</i>) + 1.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does f(x) mean?",
   "a": "It is read “f of x” and means the output of the function f for the input x. It does not mean f times x."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you evaluate a function?",
   "a": "Replace every x in the rule with the input value, using parentheses for negative numbers, then simplify."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the difference between f(3) and f(x) = 3?",
   "a": "f(3) asks for the output when the input is 3. f(x) = 3 asks which input gives an output of 3."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you find f(a + 1)?",
   "a": "Replace every x with (a + 1) and simplify. For f(x) = 2x + 5, f(a + 1) = 2(a + 1) + 5 = 2a + 7."
  }
 ],
